    "content": "Mogadishu — Somali Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre participated in a ceremony on August 19, 2026, commemorating the 15th anniversary of the renewed diplomatic and fraternal relations between the Federal Republic of Somalia and the Republic of Turkey. The event underscored the deep-rooted cooperation that has evolved between the two nations since its reinvigoration in 2011.\n\nDuring his remarks, Prime Minister Barre reflected on the challenging circumstances Somalia faced 15 years ago, when the country was grappling with severe drought, famine, and widespread insecurity. He noted that Turkey's engagement during that critical period marked a turning point, as Ankara extended vital support that helped lay the groundwork for Somalia's gradual stabilization and reconstruction.\n\nOver the past 15 years, bilateral ties have expanded significantly, encompassing areas such as infrastructure development, education, health, and security cooperation. Turkey has established a major military base in Mogadishu, trained Somali security forces, and invested in key projects, while also providing humanitarian aid during emergencies. The relationship is often cited as a model for South-South cooperation, with Ankara maintaining a visible presence in Somalia despite regional and international complexities.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the strengthened Somalia-Turkey partnership carries particular significance. Turkish investments and initiatives have created employment opportunities and improved public services, which are factors that influence decisions about return and investment. Moreover, Turkey's stance on Somalia's sovereignty and territorial integrity has been reassuring for diaspora communities concerned about external interference. The stability generated by such alliances can enhance confidence among Somalis abroad who are considering contributing to their homeland's progress.\n\nAs the two countries mark this milestone, the event served as a reminder of how strategic partnerships can help nations overcome adversity. The Prime Minister's participation underscores the Somali government's commitment to deepening such ties, which remain central to the country's long-term development goals. The anniversary celebration comes at a time when Somalia continues to rebuild after decades of conflict, with Turkey standing as one of its most prominent allies.",
