    "content": "Officials from the Federal Government of Somalia and Turkish diplomats gathered in Mogadishu to mark 15 years since the two countries re-established their strategic partnership. The event, which took place in the capital, featured a review of joint projects and milestones achieved since the relationship was reinvigorated in 2011.\n\nThe partnership, which had originally been established in the 1960s, was significantly deepened in the early 2010s following a visit by Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan. Since then, Turkey has become one of Somalia's closest allies, providing humanitarian aid, infrastructure development, and military training.\n\nTurkish investments have included the reconstruction and management of Mogadishu's international airport and seaport, as well as the construction of hospitals, schools, and other public facilities. The relationship has also expanded to include security cooperation, with Turkey building a military base in Mogadishu in 2017.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the strengthened ties have meant improved travel and economic opportunities. Turkish Airlines operates regular flights to Mogadishu, facilitating easier movement for sojourners visiting family or conducting business. Additionally, Turkey's development projects have contributed to stability in the region, which benefits diaspora investments back home.\n\nAs the anniversary was celebrated with praises for past achievements, officials also expressed commitments to further enhancing bilateral relations in the coming years. The event underscored the mutual interests that continue to drive the partnership forward.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/19/muqdisho-oo-laga-xusayo-15-sano-guuradii-ka-soo-wareegtay-markii-uu-dib-u-xoogaystay-xariika-soomaaliya-iyo-turkigu/",
