    "id": "news-1787120804625-0",
    "title": "Former Somali President Criticizes Federal Government Over Baidoa Clashes",
    "original_title": "Somalia ex-president blames govt for Baidoa bloodshed that left 30 dead",
    "slug": "former-somali-president-criticizes-federal-government-over-baidoa-clashes",
    "summary": "Former President Sharif Sheikh Ahmed blamed the federal government for recent deadly clashes in Baidoa, citing a forcible change of power in March. At least 30 people were killed in the violence.",
    "content": "MOGADISHU — Somalia's former President Sharif Sheikh Ahmed has accused the federal government under President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud of being responsible for the recent outbreak of fighting in Baidoa, the capital of Southwest State. In a statement issued on Tuesday, he argued that the violence was a direct outcome of a power transition carried out through force in March of this year.\n\nThe clashes, which took place on Monday, resulted in at least 30 deaths, according to local reports. Sharif described the incident as tragic and expressed that he and other opposition leaders, as well as any person with a conscience, would continually denounce such acts of bloodshed. He did not provide specific evidence to support his claim of government involvement but reiterated his stance that the federal administration's actions had exacerbated instability in the region.\n\nBackground context indicates that tensions have been high in Southwest State since March, when a new leadership was installed amid accusations of irregular procedures and the use of force. This change has been a point of contention between the federal government and opposition figures, who view it as an infringement on regional autonomy and democratic processes. The recent escalation in Baidoa is seen as part of a broader pattern of political instability that has plagued parts of Somalia.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this news holds significant relevance as many maintain close ties to their homeland and are concerned about the safety of relatives and the prospects for peace. The recurring conflict in regions like Southwest State highlights the fragile nature of Somalia's political reconciliation and the need for inclusive dialogue among all stakeholders.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-ex-president-blames-govt-baidoa-bloodshed-30-dead/",
