    "content": "Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, during a public event in the town of Gaalkacyo on Wednesday, called for an urgent shift in how the country conducts its elections. He stated that Somalia has relied on an indirect voting model for close to two decades—a system he said has created political limitations and obstacles.\n\nSpeaking to a gathering of officials, elders, and residents, the President reviewed the various electoral stages Somalia has experienced since the collapse of the central state in 1991. He noted that while earlier phases helped stabilize the country, they fell short of giving citizens a direct voice in choosing their leaders.\n\nHis comments come as the federal government pushes forward with plans to implement universal suffrage in upcoming polls. Previous attempts to hold direct elections have faced delays due to security concerns, disagreements among political stakeholders, and logistical hurdles, but the President insisted that the time for change is now.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this shift holds particular significance. Many diaspora members have participated in indirect processes through clan delegates, but direct elections would allow broader participation and could strengthen the legitimacy of the government in their homeland. Improved electoral credibility may also encourage greater engagement from Somalis abroad in political and economic rebuilding efforts.\n\nThe President's remarks signal his administration's commitment to electoral reform, though observers note that consensus building and security improvements will be critical to realizing this goal. No timeline was announced at the event, but the discussion adds momentum to ongoing national dialogue on democratic transition.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/19/madaxweyne-xasan-sheekh-soomaaliya-waxay-u-baahan-tahay-in-loo-gudbo-doorasho-qof-iyo-cod-ah/",
